# Market Terminal: project brief

Source of truth for requirements. Written 2026-09-24 from the working sessions with César.
Rules for data and style live in `CLAUDE.md`; this file says *what* to build.

## Goal

A personal, institutional-grade markets system that runs mostly unattended:
market data from LSEG Workspace plus free public sources, arithmetic done in code,
prose written by an LLM around numbers it never invents.

## Constraints

- LSEG Workspace desktop (proxy `localhost:9000`) is guaranteed open at **16:00 CDMX**. US close is 14:00 CDMX. LSEG jobs run at or after 16:00.
- Free and public sources first; LSEG is the paid exception. Minimize API calls and compute (cache, incremental pulls).
- Text generation: Claude Haiku through `claude -p --model haiku` on César's subscription (no API key). The model receives JSON and returns prose; numbers are injected by code.
- Scheduling runs locally (Windows Task Scheduler or the desktop app's local scheduled tasks), because cloud jobs cannot reach `localhost:9000`.
- Deliverables in English, Arial, IB/corporate style, no em-dashes, few colons.
- Design system: `engine/paleta.md` + `engine/grafico_banquero.py`. The dashboard supports **dark and light themes with a switch**; every chart must read in both.

## Surfaces

### 1. Market dashboard (main screen)

Bloomberg TV-style arrangement (reference screenshots in the session, 2026-09-24):

- **Main panel** (where Bloomberg shows video): rotating relevant charts chosen by the detector (anomalies, divergences, 52-week extremes).
- **Below it**: "What it implies" and the insight for the chart on screen.
- **News block**: market-moving headlines from the whitelisted sources, ranked, with related stories.
- **Right column**: ticker tiles with last, change, % and intraday sparkline: major indices, selected single stocks, FX (EURUSD, USDMXN, ...), rates, commodities, VIX. Only verified RICs (`.DJI` is not licensed; use DIA). Crypto only if a verified source exists.
- Market only. Portfolio has its own page.
- Links to: deck library, Ideas Screener, 13F tracker, Portfolio.

### 2. Decks

- **Daily** (after 16:00 CDMX): 1-2 slides, market + portfolio.
- **Weekly** (Friday close): full deck on `templates/News September.pptx`:
  1. Cover "Market ..." + period
  2. General market: main indices and why they moved (VIX up: why; S&P down: why)
  3. S&P 500 by sector with deltas and reasons (arithmetic attribution: sector contribution in pp)
  4. Sector multiples chart (harmonic, from constituents)
  5. Macro + economic calendar (charts on one side, items listed vertically); rate-hike probabilities from fed funds futures
  6. Mexico section (IPC, Banxico, local M&A such as tender offers)
  7. Thematic: AI (forward P/E of WFE cluster KLA / AMAT / LRCX / TSMC vs 2-year group average; Memory: SK Hynix, Micron, Samsung; hyperscalers; buildout/power)
  8. Portfolio moves and why (name contribution to portfolio return)
  9. Portfolio forward P/E per name + blended (harmonic)
  10. Closing slide
- Decks are listed and downloadable in the dashboard and exported to the OneDrive folder in `config.SALIDA_DECKS`.
- Existing chart generators in `engine/` are the building blocks; none of them assembles slides yet.

### 3. Ideas Screener (refresh every ~15 days)

- Universe: listings in the **US, Europe and Japan only**.
- Filters:
  - Limited revenue and customer exposure to Emerging Markets (needs geographic segment data; verify field availability).
  - Net debt / EBITDA below 3x (hard ceiling 4x).
  - Revenue CAGR positive and rising.
  - Margins better than GICS peers, **or** a turnaround with improving fundamentals.
- Output columns: Company, Sector, Currency, Market Cap (USD), YTD, 3M, ROE, Net Margin LTM, Net Debt / EBITDA, Fwd P/E (plus the peer-comparison columns from César's AZTD_Universe workbook: gross/EBIT/EBITDA margin, FCF conversion, FCF yield, capex % revenue, revenue growth last Q, revenue CAGR next 2Y, dividend). Reuse the `TR.F.*` codes proven in that workbook.
- Bundles: **Small < US$10B, Mid < US$50B, Large >= US$50B**.
- Peer average row per group (excluding the name itself), heat-colored against peers.
- Deep dive per name, in the style of the WFE/portfolio analyses (forward P/E vs 2Y range, EPS revisions vs multiple, peers).

### 4. Portfolio

- Ingest IBKR Activity Statements / transaction files by dropping them in a folder; new files are detected and read. Port the V3 engine (FIFO, TWR, MWR, risk, Markowitz) and fix its multi-header parser bug.
- Performance refreshed every **15 minutes** (yfinance intraday; LSEG at the close).
- **Constant-FX exercise** and **MXN view**: return in pesos using the actual USDMXN of each deposit vs today, and at constant FX.
- **Commissions**, discreet panel: total paid, % of traded value, per trade.
- **Portfolio insights**: what moved it and why (news per holding), estimate revisions vs multiple change.
- **Rivals**: 3 GICS peers per holding, their moves and news.
- Bloomberg-style portfolio page (same layout grammar as the market dashboard).

### 5. 13F tracker (quarterly)

- Funds: AI-focused and infrastructure-focused managers with 13F holdings above US$30B (13F reports holdings value, not AUM; this is the proxy). Seed list to be approved by César.
- Source: SEC EDGAR (free; User-Agent contact in `.env`). Prefer SEC's quarterly Form 13F structured datasets over scraping each filer.
- Bot downloads new filings after each deadline (45 days after quarter end), computes QoQ allocation changes (new, exited, increased, trimmed, by weight), and flags overlaps with the portfolio and screener.
- Own section in the dashboard; highlights feed the weekly deck in filing months. No politicians.

## News and documents (all surfaces)

What César wants from news: **the headline, a link to open it, and only what is relevant and important**. Important includes documents, not just articles: central bank speeches and papers, working papers, and the PDFs that banks and asset managers publish.

Every item carries:

| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| Headline | As published, never rewritten by the LLM |
| Source + tier | From the whitelist in `engine/noticias.py` (primary, specialized, press, analysis) |
| Timestamp | Source time, shown in CDMX |
| Type | Article, PDF, paper, filing, speech |
| Link | External URL when the source has one (RSS, SEC, central banks). LSEG stories have no confirmed public URL, so they open in a local story page served by the backend (`get_story` HTML, cached once fetched) |
| Why it is here | The match that let it through: holding, GICS rival, theme (AI/WFE/memory/power), macro (Fed/ECB/Banxico/CPI/jobs), Mexico, 13F fund |

Relevance filter, in order:
1. Source is on the whitelist (unlisted = excluded) and the headline is not robot noise (`RUIDO` regex).
2. It matches an entity or theme above. No match, no item.
3. Duplicates of the same event collapse into one item, keeping the highest-tier source; the others become "related stories".
4. Rank: source tier, then link to a detector finding (a news item that explains an anomaly on screen goes first), then recency.
5. Hard cap per block, so the page stays scannable.

